T* (The Republic of Plato is the longest of his works with the exception of) Tj
T* (the Laws, and is certainly the greatest of them. There are nearer) Tj
T* (approaches to modern metaphysics in the Philebus and in the Sophist;) Tj
T* (the Politicus or Statesman is more ideal; the form and institutions of) Tj
T* (the State are more clearly drawn out in the Laws; as works of art, the) Tj
T* (Symposium and the Protagoras are of higher excellence. But no other) Tj
T* (Dialogue of Plato has the same largeness of view and the same) Tj
T* (perfection of style; no other shows an equal knowledge of the world, or) Tj
T* (contains more of those thoughts which are new as well as old, and not) Tj
T* (of one age only but of all. Nowhere in Plato is there a deeper irony or) Tj
T* (a greater wealth of humour or imagery, or more dramatic power. Nor in) Tj
