T* (fell near him, all but Er himself \(he was not allowed\), and each as he) Tj
T* (took his lot perceived the number which he had obtained. Then the) Tj
T* (Interpreter placed on the ground before them the samples of lives; and) Tj
T* (there were many more lives than the souls present, and they were of all) Tj
T* (sorts. There were lives of every animal and of man in every condition.) Tj
T* (And there were tyrannies among them, some lasting out the tyrant?s) Tj
T* (life, others which broke off in the middle and came to an end in) Tj
T* (poverty and exile and beggary; and there were lives of famous men, some) Tj
T* (who were famous for their form and beauty as well as for their strength) Tj
T* (and success in games, or, again, for their birth and the qualities of) Tj
T* (their ancestors; and some who were the reverse of famous for the) Tj
T* (opposite qualities. And of women likewise; there was not, however, any) Tj
T* (definite character in them, because the soul, when choosing a new life,) Tj
T* (must of necessity become different. But there was every other quality,) Tj
T* (and the all mingled with one another, and also with elements of wealth) Tj
T* (and poverty, and disease and health; and there were mean states also.) Tj
T* (And here, my dear Glaucon, is the supreme peril of our human state; and) Tj
T* (therefore the utmost care should be taken. Let each one of us leave) Tj
T* (every other kind of knowledge and seek and follow one thing only, if) Tj
T* (peradventure he may be able to learn and may find some one who will) Tj
T* (make him able to learn and discern between good and evil, and so to) Tj
T* (choose always and everywhere the better life as he has opportunity. He) Tj
T* (should consider the bearing of all these things which have been) Tj
T* (mentioned severally and collectively upon virtue; he should know what) Tj
T* (the effect of beauty is when combined with poverty or wealth in a) Tj
T* (particular soul, and what are the good and evil consequences of noble) Tj
T* (and humble birth, of private and public station, of strength and) Tj
T* (weakness, of cleverness and dullness, and of all the natural and) Tj
T* (acquired gifts of the soul, and the operation of them when conjoined;) Tj
T* (he will then look at the nature of the soul, and from the consideration) Tj
T* (of all these qualities he will be able to determine which is the better) Tj
T* (and which is the worse; and so he will choose, giving the name of evil) Tj
T* (to the life which will make his soul more unjust, and good to the life) Tj
T* (which will make his soul more just; all else he will disregard. For we) Tj
T* (have seen and know that this is the best choice both in life and after) Tj
T* (death. A man must take with him into the world below an adamantine) Tj
T* (faith in truth and right, that there too he may be undazzled by the) Tj
T* (desire of wealth or the other allurements of evil, lest, coming upon) Tj
T* (tyrannies and similar villainies, he do irremediable wrongs to others) Tj
T* (and suffer yet worse himself; but let him know how to choose the mean) Tj
T* (and avoid the extremes on either side, as far as possible, not only in) Tj
T* (this life but in all that which is to come. For this is the way of) Tj
ET
