T* (And according to the report of the messenger from the other world this) Tj
T* (was what the prophet said at the time: ?Even for the last comer, if he) Tj
T* (chooses wisely and will live diligently, there is appointed a happy and) Tj
T* (not undesirable existence. Let not him who chooses first be careless,) Tj
T* (and let not the last despair.? And when he had spoken, he who had the) Tj
T* (first choice came forward and in a moment chose the greatest tyranny;) Tj
T* (his mind having been darkened by folly and sensuality, he had not) Tj
T* (thought out the whole matter before he chose, and did not at first) Tj
T* (sight perceive that he was fated, among other evils, to devour his own) Tj
T* (children. But when he had time to reflect, and saw what was in the lot,) Tj
T* (he began to beat his breast and lament over his choice, forgetting the) Tj
T* (proclamation of the prophet; for, instead of throwing the blame of his) Tj
T* (misfortune on himself, he accused chance and the gods, and everything) Tj
T* (rather than himself. Now he was one of those who came from heaven, and) Tj
T* (in a former life had dwelt in a well-ordered State, but his virtue was) Tj
T* (a matter of habit only, and he had no philosophy. And it was true of) Tj
T* (others who were similarly overtaken, that the greater number of them) Tj
T* (came from heaven and therefore they had never been schooled by trial,) Tj
T* (whereas the pilgrims who came from earth having themselves suffered and) Tj
T* (seen others suffer, were not in a hurry to choose. And owing to this) Tj
T* (inexperience of theirs, and also because the lot was a chance, many of) Tj
T* (the souls exchanged a good destiny for an evil or an evil for a good.) Tj
T* (For if a man had always on his arrival in this world dedicated himself) Tj
T* (from the first to sound philosophy, and had been moderately fortunate) Tj
T* (in the number of the lot, he might, as the messenger reported, be happy) Tj
T* (here, and also his journey to another life and return to this, instead) Tj
T* (of being rough and underground, would be smooth and heavenly. Most) Tj
T* (curious, he said, was the spectacle?sad and laughable and strange; for) Tj
T* (the choice of the souls was in most cases based on their experience of) Tj
T* (a previous life. There he saw the soul which had once been Orpheus) Tj
T* (choosing the life of a swan out of enmity to the race of women, hating) Tj
T* (to be born of a woman because they had been his murderers; he beheld) Tj
T* (also the soul of Thamyras choosing the life of a nightingale; birds, on) Tj
T* (the other hand, like the swan and other musicians, wanting to be men.) Tj
T* (The soul which obtained the twentieth lot chose the life of a lion, and) Tj
T* (this was the soul of Ajax the son of Telamon, who would not be a man,) Tj
T* (remembering the injustice which was done him in the judgment about the) Tj
T* (arms. The next was Agamemnon, who took the life of an eagle, because,) Tj
T* (like Ajax, he hated human nature by reason of his sufferings. About the) Tj
T* (middle came the lot of Atalanta; she, seeing the great fame of an) Tj
T* (athlete, was unable to resist the temptation: and after her there) Tj
T* (followed the soul of Epeus the son of Panopeus passing into the nature) Tj
